<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Hanifa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">699–767</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Abū Ḥanīfa, Nu'man</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Hanifa was a Muslim scholar, jurist and theologian, the founder and eponym of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> jurisprudence. After studying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> he succeeded his teacher Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman as the leading authority on Islamic law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, and his school was distinguished by its use of analogy (qiyas), juristic preference (istihsan) and personal judgement (ra'y). He refused the post of chief judge offered by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur in 763, was imprisoned and tortured, and died in prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 767. Through his students Abu Yusuf and Muhammad al-Shaybani his school spread, and it remains the most widely practised of the Sunni schools.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-hanifa.jpg" alt="Abu Hanifa" width="330" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maruziyan 21:07, 18. Nov. 2006 (CET) · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grab_hanifa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Hanifa came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, though sources disagree on his birthplace, naming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> (the majority view), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, Anbar, Nasa or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/termez">Termez</a>. They agree that he was born under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> but differ on the year: 699 CE, held by the majority of classical scholarship, or 696, 689 or 680. His first name was Nu'man. He was born at least 60 years after the death of Muhammad, when some of the first generation of Muslims were still alive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his youth Abu Hanifa travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, and he later became established as a leading religious authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>. One of his main teachers there was Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman. He never held an official post as qadi. He kept an independent position toward political authority and was punished and imprisoned under both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> because of his views and his refusal to accept the office of qadi in Kufa. He died in prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 767 (AH 150).</p><p>The scholar Yusuf ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Mizzi listed 97 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> scholars among his students, and Badr al-Din al-Ayni named another 260 who studied hadith and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">fiqh</a> with him. His two best-known students were Abu Yusuf, who served as the first chief justice in the Muslim world, and Muhammad al-Shaybani, who taught the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i school</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shafii">al-Shafi'i</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Abu Hanifa derived Islamic law, in order of preference, from the Qur'an, the authentic narrations of Muhammad known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, the consensus of the Muslim community (ijma'), analogical reasoning (qiyas), juristic discretion (istihsan) and local custom ('<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urf">urf</a>). Modern scholarship regards him as the first to formally adopt analogical reasoning as part of Islamic law. Because Ali had moved the Islamic capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and many first-generation Muslims settled there, the school came to be known as the Kufan or Iraqi school.</p><p>His school, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a>, remains the most widely practised of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> schools. Abu Hanifa received the honorific al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> al-A'zam, the highly venerated Imam. His tomb, surmounted by a dome erected by admirers in 1066, is still a shrine for pilgrims; the Ottoman ruler Suleiman restored it in 1535 after the Ottoman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Hanifa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In these two centuries they built the schools of law named after <b>Abu Hanifa</b>, Malik, al-Shafi’i and Ahmad ibn Hanbal.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>The caliph al-Mansur then had supporters such as <b>Abu Hanifa</b> imprisoned.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-14">14 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.03,44.4&amp;year=743" aria-label="Kufa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5693 998 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abu Hanifa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6143 1268h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6143 1268h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.03,44.4&amp;year=743">What was here in 743 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
